-- title:
--   Preparing a tail threshold before a candidate is chosen
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. The tree is built level by level from candidate records: each candidate is a generated map attached to a parent node together with a norm-one functional chosen after the map, and every candidate, including those that later receive coefficient zero, consumes a global ordinal $n$ with budget $2^{-(n+9)}$. Fix normalized minimal data, a finite frontier, earlier candidate parents and points, base points, an ultrafilter, a dense-prefix length $k$, finitely many generated ancestor maps $G_a$, and $e>0$.
--
--   1. **Prepared threshold.** There is $m\ge1$ such that every map $g$ in the tail family of order $m$ is generated. Moreover, for every input $x$ there is $f$ with $\|f\|\le1$ and $f(g(x)-z)\ge1-e$ for all $z$ in the prediction detector set, and
--   $$
--   \sum_a\|G_a(g(x))-g(x)\|\le e .
--   $$
--   The threshold is fixed before the candidate map and its input are chosen.
